// @no-instantiate
// MISCOMPILE, pinned: an arm-shaped object literal that reaches its DECLARED RESULT through an
// element-preserving LIST conduit — `reverse([c])[0]` — in a program that also declares a
// standalone struct of the arm's exact layout. Ten lines, one import, no lambda, no `??`, one
// nesting level.
//
// `vl check` exits 0 with NO diagnostics at all and is the ONLY command that misses it;
// `vl check --codegen` exits 1, `vl check --codegen --no-validate` exits 0, `vl build` writes
// the `.wasm` and exits 1, `vl run` exits 1:
//
//   the emitted module is not valid wasm … type mismatch:
//   expected (ref $type), found (ref $type)
//
// (No byte offset quoted on purpose: it moves with the seed. The two `$type`s are the arm's
//  variant-table heap and the twin's struct-table heap, which `DECISIONS.md` keeps in two
//  namespaces on purpose: "the variant/struct-TABLE seam stays nominal".)
//
// IT IS NOT STD-SPECIFIC, AND THAT IS MEASURED. The same program with `reverse` replaced by a
// hand-written `function rv<T>(xs: T[]): T[] { return xs }` — nine lines, NO import — is the
// identical failure. What the conduit has to be is ELEMENT-PRESERVING: a callee whose result
// annotation is spelled exactly as one of its parameters' and whose spelling mentions a type
// parameter, read back with an index.
//
// EIGHT CONTROLS, all built and RUN against the tree this file ships with — never reasoned,
// and identical on `1559d80c` and on this tree:
//
//   (as filed)                                      -> check-clean invalid wasm
//   `return c` (delete the conduit)                 -> RUNS
//   annotate `const c: Circle`                      -> RUNS
//   delete `type Dot`                               -> RUNS
//   `type Dot = { q: i32 }` (a non-twin)            -> RUNS
//   delete `type Shape = Circle | Sq`               -> RUNS
//   a hand-written `rv<T>(xs: T[]): T[]` conduit    -> check-clean invalid wasm
//   bind the list first: `const xs: Circle[] = [c]` -> RUNS
//
// THE EXACT LAYOUT TWIN IS REQUIRED (controls 4 and 5 both RUN — unlike D139/D155, where the
// twin-free spellings were LOUD), and THE LAST CONTROL IS THE ONE THAT NAMES THE SECOND ROOT:
// annotating the LIST makes it run, so what the emitter is missing is the list literal's
// element row, not only the pin.
//
// TWO ROOTS, AND BOTH WERE BUILT. The first is the pin's: `dstPinCallArgs` treats a parameter
// as an open generic position only when its spelling IS a type parameter, so `reverse`'s
// `xs: T[]` read as a CONCRETE destination and pushed `""` — a DISAGREEMENT that vetoed the
// pin the declared result was carrying — and `dstPinSrcIs` cannot see through an `Index` over
// a `Call` over an `ArrayLit` anyway. Both halves were written and measured (see
// silent-class-inventory D157) and NEITHER IS IN THIS TREE: together they make the pin fire
// with the right name and move 0 cells, because the SECOND root then keeps every one of them
// silent. That second root is D163 — the array literal's element row is re-derived from the
// checker's structural record and disagrees with the element's committed rep.
//
// PRE-EXISTING on `1559d80c`, same message — not a regression from the change this file ships
// with. Filed as `silent-class-inventory.md` D157.
//
// IT IS THE SPECIMEN. This file is kept byte-for-byte identical to
// `tests/vl_check_codegen_test.ts`'s `INVALID_MODULE_SRC` below its header, which needs a
// program that type-checks clean and whose module the engine refuses.
//
// WHEN IT IS FIXED this file starts failing (the module instantiates), which is the point:
// delete it, graduate a `@run` row, and swap `INVALID_MODULE_SRC` for another live member of
// the class. READ THE NOTE IN `tests/vl_check_codegen_test.ts` BEFORE DOING THAT — including
// what it says about where to look before concluding the class is empty. Three more live
// members are filed with runnable witnesses as D156, D158 and D163.
import { reverse } from "std:array"
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
type Dot = { r: i32 }
function mk(n: i32): Circle {
  const c = { r: n }
  return reverse([c])[0]
}
print((mk(7)).r)
